UNIT adopt_replace_block
LANG js
PRIMARY-SHA256 e6eeba5909802787e54f046addbade224098d3b0b3de51c2fb1aa0cc6d286cb5
SIG adopt_replace_block(existing: string, block: { heading: string, startLine: number, endLine: number }, managed: string, kept: string[]): string
NOTE default export
ABI text = string (UTF-16 code units; well-formed inside the envelope); i64 = number, a safe integer; record = a plain
  object with the three named own properties; list[text] = Array of string. A throw (R2, R3) is a thrown Error; its
  message is not graded. The artifact is an ES module default-exporting the function; it imports nothing.
MECHANISM R1 is String.prototype.split with the one-character separator "\n" (lines keep their CR). WS is the
  String.prototype.trim set: R9 is trim(). R3 and R10 test the VISIBLE pin (u flag: astral CF characters are one code
  point; \s under u is exactly WS). R4 tests the CRLF pin on the outside lines joined with "\n". R5 is the BLANK pin, R8
  the CONVERT pin with a global replace, R10's tail strip the KEPT_END pin.
DEPENDS-ON none
PINNED regex VISIBLE = /[^\s\p{Cf}]/u CITED R3 R10 — \p{Cf} is the engine's Unicode; the primary's CF pin (Unicode 17.0) is the truth where an older engine differs
PINNED regex BLANK = /^[ \t\r]*$/ CITED R5 — no m flag; tested on one line
PINNED regex CRLF = /\r\n/ CITED R4 — tested on lines 0..S-1 and E..N-1 joined with "\n"
PINNED regex CONVERT = /\r\n|\r|\n/g CITED R8 — each match replaced by EOL; the alternation order makes CR LF one match
PINNED regex KEPT_END = /[\r\n]+$/ CITED R10 — replaced by the empty text
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/lib/adopt.mts — this file states the type mapping and the dialect only; no behavior.
